+rescaling. Those operations are not polynomial moment closures.
+
+Homogeneous Stage-2 evidence shows why this distinction is material. The raw
+source remains realizable but develops a source norm above `5e10` near
+`t=0.025`; an adaptive explicit step then requires `h/dt < 2^-24`. This is a
+high-moment runaway of the unclipped closure, not merely the original
+integrator's 256-substep cap.
+
+## Bounded quadrature model
+
+`fp_collision_source35_bounded` evaluates the nonlinear drift directly on the
+non-negative CHyQMOM nodes. It applies the same
+`min(|c|^2, speed_cap*theta)` limiter as FPCode, with `speed_cap=25` by default.
+This direct evaluation is required because a clipped radial function cannot be
+recovered exactly from a finite M5/M6 polynomial lookup.
+
+The bounded source also includes the continuous limits of the two invariant
+corrections used by the homogeneous particle reference. If `N(c)` denotes the
+nonlinear drift, it subtracts its quadrature mean to conserve momentum and
+adds
+
+```text
+-lambda*c,   lambda = <c dot N> / (3*theta),
+```
+
+which is the first-order-in-time limit of FPCode's finite-step `alpha`
+rescaling. This correction acts consistently on all retained moments, unlike
+changing only the diagonal second-moment source after projection. A final
+low-order invariant projection removes quadrature roundoff only.
+
+The raw analytical function remains available as a diagnostic control. The
+bounded function is the candidate for the next homogeneous comparison, not yet
+a production solver choice.
 
